title: Interdisciplinary Studies SBA, Part 3 - High School Dropout Analysis
authors: EDUC 3100 Assessment Module
~~~~

Orientation [id=orientation3]
-----------

Omer: Welcome back to the Interdisciplinary Research Process (IRP) Scenario-based Activity!

Part 3 should take around 45 minutes to an hour, and you can break it up over several days if you'd like. **It is due December 7**.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_a -> interdisciplinary_3_clear

Lianne: You've done good work so far. Let's recap where we are. We've found studies that address different parts of the high school dropout problem, including Psychology, Sociology, Economics, and Political Science.

Lianne: This confirms that this is a complex problem with parts studied by different disciplines.

Lianne: Then we mapped them for an interdisciplinary understanding. That allowed us to look for missing insights in policy reform. We located one. When you continue, you will see to the right the final version of our map followed by the summary of interdisciplinary connections.

::extended_summary_3 [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_a label="Review your summary of your work so far."]

Lianne: We now have a broad and more comprehensive understanding of the problem.

The Importance and Unresolved Status of the Issue [id=interdisciplinary_status]
-----------

Lacey: If I'm remembering Repko correctly, now we need to see if this is an important and as yet unresolved issue. That's also an important criterion for a problem to qualify for interdisciplinary study.

Ty: Then let's see if we can find any sources that address that criterion.

interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b -> research_study_dropout_trends

Peggy: I think this source will help us.

Lianne: This would be a good time to use the highlighting method to look for information about the **importance** of this problem to the individual, society, and country.

_Click continue to apply the highlighting method to this source. Make sure that you add notes to each item you highlight explaining how it speaks to the importance of the issue._

::research_study_dropout_trends_annotated [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b label="Annotate information on the importance of the problem."]

Lianne: Now let's go back over the text and highlight places that help us see that the high school dropout problem is **not yet resolved**. Make sure to add notes explaining how the highlighted text indicates that this is an ongoing problem.

::research_study_dropout_trends_annotated [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b label="Highlight and annotate text that shows the problem is not yet resolved."]

Peggy: I've put together a sentence that explains why dropping out of high school is an important and unresolved problem. Maybe we can polish it up and use it as a part of our problem statement.

::peggys_significance_statement [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b label="View Peggy's draft significance statement."]

Lacy: That doesn't sound right. It focuses too much on crime and vocational education. We should rewrite it so that it more accurately reflects what we've learned so far.

Lianne: Good catch, Lacy. We need to avoid **disciplinary or personal bias** so that we can develop a comprehensive understanding of the causes of the problem without privileging any personal or disciplinary views over others. According to Repko et al. (2019), this is one of the criteria for doing interdisciplinary work.

Can we correct and revise this sentence based on the source texts we have reviewed?

::correct_significance_statement [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b label="Correct Peggy's draft significance statement."]

Peggy: I can see that my sentence needed to be fixed. Thanks for the suggested revision. I took your suggestions into account and revised my original significance statement.

::peggys_revised_significance_statement [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_b label="View Peggy's revised significance statement."]

Lianne: OK, as we move closer to developing a problem statement, let's pull together all of the information
we've gathered. Take some time to review everything, and then we'l start figuring out how to write the 
problem statement.

Criteria for an Effective Problem Statement [id=interdisciplinary_problem_statement]
-----------

::repko_criteria [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_c label="View Repko's Criteria for a successful problem statement."]

Lianne: At this point in our interdisciplinary process, we need to develop a problem statement
on the causes of high school dropout. We will use Repko's (2019) criteria for writing and
effective problem statement.

Peggy: We have already described why high school dropout is an important problem (Criterion \#1)
and talked about its complexity (Criterion \#2). We also talked about the importance of avoiding
personal or disciplinary biases in the interdisciplinary research process (Criterion \#3).

Lianne: That is right. As for Criteria \#4-5, we need to tie the focus (\#4) and scope (\#5) criteria
to what our nonprofit can do considering our priorities and capacity.

Ty: That makes sense. Can you remind us what the nonprofit's priorities are?

Lianne: Sure. Take a look! 

(_hit continue to view_).

::comm360_mission [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_c label="View Comm360's mission statement."]

Lianne: Hit continue, then make your choices in the table to the right.

You may feel that all or none of the choices apply to a specific cause. Feel free to check all that apply but
be ready to explain how you see the cause can be the basis for your choices. I'll need those explanations
to bring to the committee.



::cause_service_mapping [display=target:interdisciplinary_sba_sidebar3_c label="Map causes of high school dropout to Comm360 Service Areas"]

